OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026", "title": "A Counterexample to Kaplansky's Direct-Finiteness Conjecture in Odd Characteristic", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "197", "family_title": "A torsion-free group algebra that is not directly finite", "abstract_html": "We construct a counterexample to Kaplansky's direct-finiteness conjecture in odd characteristic. For one specified odd prime <i>p</i>, we obtain a field <i>K</i> of order <i>p</i><sup>4</sup>, a finitely generated group <i>G</i> containing torsion, and finite sums \\(a,b\\in K[G]\\) with \\(ab=1\\) but \\(ba\\ne1\\). The same elements define a cellular automaton on <i>K</i><sup><i>G</i></sup> that is injective but not surjective.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  m=\\binom{1200}{600},\n  \\qquad\n  p=\\min\\{h\\in\\mathbb Z:h&gt;1,\\ h\\mid (m!)^2+1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A group algebra \\(K[G]\\) consists of finite sums \\(\\sum_g c_g g\\), multiplied using the group law and bilinearity.</p><p class=\"li\">“Specified” means given by finite formulas and choices in ordered finite sets. It does not mean that the construction has been evaluated as a practical computation.</p><p class=\"li\">All matrices act on columns of free right modules; matrix entries multiply coordinates on the left. Transpose over \\(K\\) is ordinary transpose, without an involution. All cardinalities and dimensions used in rank comparisons are integers.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The integer \\(p\\) is an odd prime. There are a specified field \\(K\\) of order \\(p^4\\), a specified finitely generated group \\(G\\) containing torsion, and specified finite sums \\(a,b\\in K[G]\\) satisfying\n\\[\nab=1,\\qquad ba\\ne1.\n\\]\nThe group and sums are defined without solving the word problem of \\(G\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 supplies the specified odd prime \\(p&gt;m\\), the field \\(K\\) of order \\(p^4\\), and the element \\(\\zeta\\) of order \\(M=p^4-1\\).</p><p>Proposition 2 gives the triangle-free graph and specified fitting factorization with\n\\[\n\\frac tm&lt;\\frac1{100}.\n\\]\nProposition 3 gives the specified local idempotent \\(e\\in K[H]\\), whose positive regular rank \\(d\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac dw&gt;\\frac1{96}.\n\\]\nProposition 4 gives the ordered products \\(e_ie_j=0\\) on edges \\(i&lt;j\\). Proposition 6 embeds each \\(H_i\\) in \\(G_0\\), so these local groups and their group algebras are retained.</p><p>The group \\(G_0\\) is countable because it is finitely generated by the finitely many finite groups \\(H_i\\). Apply Proposition 7 to these embedded groups and identical local idempotents. It gives \\(G_1\\), the nonzero central idempotent \\(f\\), the ring\n\\[\nS=fK[G_1],\n\\]\nand matrices \\(J_i,Y_i\\) with\n\\[\nY_iJ_i=fI_d,\\qquad J_iY_i=P_iI_w,\n\\qquad P_i=fe_i.\n\\]\nThe ordered edge products give \\(P_iP_j=0\\) for edges \\(i&lt;j\\).</p><p>For distinct nonedges, \\(F_{ij}=0\\); for edges \\(i&lt;j\\), \\(P_iP_j=0\\). Thus\n\\[\nF_{ij}P_iP_j=0\\qquad(i&lt;j).\n\\]\nProposition 8, with identity \\(f\\) in \\(S\\), provides\n\\[\nC^\\sharp\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{wt\\times md}(S),\n\\qquad\nD^\\sharp\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{md\\times wt}(S),\n\\qquad\nD^\\sharp C^\\sharp=fI_{md}.\n\\]\nThe strict rational inequalities are\n\\[\n\\frac tm&lt;\\frac1{100}&lt;\\frac1{96}&lt;\\frac dw.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nr:=wt&lt;md=:N.\n\\]</p><p>Apply Proposition 9 with \\(R=K[G_1]\\), \\(C=C^\\sharp\\), and \\(D=D^\\sharp\\). It gives explicitly specified\n\\[\nA_R,B_R\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_r(K[G_1])\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nA_RB_R=I_r,\\qquad B_RA_R\\ne I_r.\n\\]\nThe defect is certified by\n\\[\nv_*=C^\\sharp u_*\\ne0,\n\\qquad\nD^\\sharp v_*=u_*,\n\\qquad\n(B_RA_R-I_r)v_*=-v_*\\ne0.\n\\]\nIts nonvanishing is rooted in \\(f\\ne0\\), which follows from the exact order of \\(z\\); it does not require deciding equality of group words.</p><p>Apply Proposition 12 with \\(L=G_1\\), \\(M=p^4-1\\), and \\(A=A_R,B=B_R\\). It produces the finite group \\(\\Lambda\\), the group\n\\[\nG=G_1\\times\\Lambda,\n\\]\nand the specified scalar sums\n\\[\na=\n1\\otimes(1-f')\n+\n\\sum_{u,v\\in Z_q}\\widetilde A_{R,uv}\\otimes\\Omega_{uv},\n\\]\n\\[\nb=\n1\\otimes(1-f')\n+\n\\sum_{u,v\\in Z_q}\\widetilde B_{R,uv}\\otimes\\Omega_{uv},\n\\]\nsatisfying \\(ab=1\\) and \\(ba\\ne1\\). Injectivity of the matrix encoding preserves the nonzero reverse defect.</p><p>The group \\(G_0\\) is generated by the finite groups \\(H_i\\). The construction of \\(G_1\\) adds only \\(\\langle z\\rangle\\) and finitely many finite groups \\(L_i\\), and \\(\\Lambda\\) is finite. Hence \\(G\\) is finitely generated. An embedded copy of \\(H_i\\) supplies nontrivial torsion.</p><p>All choices of characters, bases, field elements, and finite inverses take place in explicitly finite ordered sets. Products of the resulting group words define finite group-algebra sums even if some words coincide. No word comparisons are needed to define the elements. The countable permutation actions certify embeddings and are not coefficient-search inputs.</p><p>The group is necessarily infinite, since every finite group algebra is finite-dimensional over its coefficient field, and a one-sided inverse between its left multiplication operators is two-sided. The construction concerns the displayed prime and retains the specified coefficient field \\(K\\) throughout.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Proposition 8.1, together with its application to the scalar construction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a finite field, \\(L\\) a group, and \\(a,b\\in k[L]\\) satisfy \\(ab=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(c=\\sum_u c_u u\\in k[L]\\), define an operator on all configurations \\(x:L\\to k\\) by\n  \\[\n  (T_cx)(g)=\\sum_u c_u x(gu).\n  \\]\n  Only the coefficient sum is required to have finite support.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(h\\in L\\), let \\(\\delta_h\\) denote the point indicator at \\(h\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(T_b:k^L\\to k^L\\) is an injective cellular automaton, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}T_b\n=\n\\{y\\in k^L:T_{ba}y=y\\}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(h\\in L\\) with \\((ba-1)_h\\ne0\\), the configuration \\(\\delta_h\\) is absent from its image.</p><p>In particular, the field, group, and scalar elements constructed in Theorem 1 give an injective, nonsurjective cellular automaton on \\(K^G\\). Thus the constructed group is not surjunctive.</p>", "proof": "<p>For arbitrary finite sums\n\\[\nc=\\sum_u c_u u,\\qquad d=\\sum_v d_v v\n\\]\nand any configuration \\(x:L\\to k\\), expanding the two finite sums gives\n\\[\n(T_cT_dx)(g)\n=\n\\sum_{u,v}c_ud_vx(guv)\n=\n(T_{cd}x)(g).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nT_cT_d=T_{cd},\n\\qquad\nT_1=\\operatorname{id}.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n(T_c\\delta_h)(1_L)=c_h,\n\\]\nwhich proves faithfulness.</p><p>The support of \\(b\\) is a finite memory set, with local rule\n\\[\n(x_u)_u\\longmapsto\\sum_u b_ux_u.\n\\]\nHence \\(T_b\\) is a cellular automaton. Since \\(ab=1\\),\n\\[\nT_aT_b=T_{ab}=\\operatorname{id},\n\\]\nso \\(T_b\\) is injective.</p><p>If \\(y=T_bx\\), then\n\\[\nT_{ba}y=T_bT_aT_bx=y.\n\\]\nConversely, if \\(T_{ba}y=y\\), then\n\\[\ny=T_b(T_ay),\n\\]\nso \\(y\\in\\operatorname{im}T_b\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}T_b=\\{y:T_{ba}y=y\\}.\n\\]</p><p>If \\((ba-1)_h\\ne0\\), then\n\\[\n(T_{ba}\\delta_h-\\delta_h)(1_L)\n=\n(ba-1)_h\\ne0.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\delta_h\\) is not a fixed point of \\(T_{ba}\\) and has no preimage under \\(T_b\\), even among configurations of infinite support.</p><p>Apply this to the scalar sums supplied by Theorem 1. Their nonzero reverse defect has a nonzero coefficient in the group basis. Hence their associated automaton is injective and nonsurjective. If the formula for \\(b\\) displays a group element more than once, its terms simply add in the same local rule; specifying the rule requires no word-problem oracle. Since \\(K\\) is finite, this proves that the constructed group is not surjunctive.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, followed by the field and ordering conventions of Section 2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  m=\\binom{1200}{600},\n  \\qquad\n  p=\\min\\{h\\in\\mathbb Z:h&gt;1,\\ h\\mid(m!)^2+1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose the least nonsquare \\(\\eta\\in\\mathbb F_p^*\\), using representatives \\(0,\\ldots,p-1\\), and put\n  \\[\n  K_0=\\mathbb F_p[\\xi]/(\\xi^2-\\eta),\n  \\qquad\n  K=K_0[\\nu]/(\\nu^2-\\xi).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The integer \\(p&gt;m\\) is an odd prime, and \\(-1\\) is a square in \\(\\mathbb F_p\\). Both displayed extensions are quadratic fields, so \\(|K|=p^4\\).</p><p>Order \\(K\\) by its coefficients in \\((1,\\xi,\\nu,\\xi\\nu)\\). Set\n\\[\nM=p^4-1\n\\]\nand choose the least \\(\\zeta\\in K^*\\) of order \\(M\\); such an element exists. The integer \\(M\\), and every power of \\(M\\), is invertible in \\(K\\).</p><p>For subsequent finite choices, use natural orders on integer indices, increasing-list lexicographic orders on subsets, and lexicographic orders on tuples and finite function tables. Order characters of \\((\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z)^E\\), for an ordered finite set \\(E\\), by their exponent lists on its ordered coordinate generators.</p>", "proof": "<p>A least divisor greater than one of an integer is prime: any nontrivial proper divisor of a composite least divisor would be smaller. If \\(p\\le m\\), then \\(p\\) divides both \\(m!\\) and \\((m!)^2+1\\), which is impossible. Hence \\(p&gt;m&gt;600\\), and the defining divisibility gives\n\\[\n(m!)^2=-1\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbb F_p.\n\\]\nThus \\(p\\) is odd and \\(-1\\) is a square.</p><p>There are nonsquares because every nonzero square has exactly two square roots. The first quotient is therefore a quadratic field. The conjugate of \\(\\xi\\) over \\(\\mathbb F_p\\) is \\(-\\xi\\), so its norm is \\(-\\eta\\). Since \\(-1\\) is square, \\(-\\eta\\) is nonsquare. A square in \\(K_0\\) would have square norm. Consequently \\(\\xi\\) is nonsquare in \\(K_0\\), and the second quotient is also a quadratic field.</p><p>To obtain \\(\\zeta\\), let \\(b\\) be the least common multiple of all element orders in \\(K^*\\). Then \\(b\\mid M\\). Every element of \\(K^*\\) is a root of \\(X^b-1\\), so the polynomial root bound gives \\(M\\le b\\). Hence \\(b=M\\). For every prime-power factor of \\(b\\), an appropriate power of an element of \\(K^*\\) has that exact order. Multiplying these commuting elements of pairwise coprime orders gives an element of order \\(b=M\\).</p><p>Finally, \\(p\\nmid M\\), so \\(M\\) and all its powers are invertible in \\(K\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 2.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(m,p,K\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">List the \\(600\\)-subsets of \\([1200]\\) as \\(I_1,\\ldots,I_m\\) in increasing-list lexicographic order.</p><p class=\"li\">Define a simple graph \\(\\Gamma\\) on \\([m]\\) by joining distinct \\(i,j\\) exactly when\n  \\[\n  |I_i\\cap I_j|&lt;200.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal T=\\{T\\subseteq[1200]:|T|\\le400\\},\n  \\qquad\n  t=|\\mathcal T|=\\sum_{j=0}^{400}\\binom{1200}{j}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A matrix \\(F\\) fits \\(\\Gamma\\) when \\(F_{ii}=1\\) and \\(F_{ij}=0\\) for distinct nonadjacent vertices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The graph \\(\\Gamma\\) is triangle-free. There are specified coefficients\n\\(\\alpha_i(T),\\beta_j(T)\\in\\mathbb F_p\\) such that\n\\[\nF_{ij}=\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal T}\\alpha_i(T)\\beta_j(T)\n\\]\nfits \\(\\Gamma\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac tm&lt;\\frac1{100}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Three pairwise adjacent sets would have union of size at least\n\\[\n3\\cdot600-3\\cdot199=1203,\n\\]\nexceeding \\(1200\\). Thus \\(\\Gamma\\) is triangle-free.</p><p>Set \\(\\alpha_i(T)=1\\) when \\(T\\subseteq I_i\\), and zero otherwise. Define \\(\\beta_j(T)\\) as the coefficient of \\(\\prod_{x\\in T}X_x\\) obtained by expanding\n\\[\nQ_j(X)=\n\\prod_{k=200}^{599}\n\\frac{\\sum_{x\\in I_j}X_x-k}{600-k}\n\\]\nand replacing every positive exponent in every monomial by one. All denominators belong to \\(\\{1,\\ldots,400\\}\\), so they are invertible in \\(\\mathbb F_p\\). This replacement preserves evaluations on zero-one vectors and does not increase degree. Consequently\n\\[\nF_{ij}\n=\n\\prod_{k=200}^{599}\n\\frac{|I_i\\cap I_j|-k}{600-k}.\n\\]\nThis equals one for \\(i=j\\). For distinct nonneighbors, the intersection size lies between \\(200\\) and \\(599\\), so one factor is zero.</p><p>The binomial coefficients increase up to index \\(400\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac tm\n\\le\n401\\prod_{u=401}^{600}\\frac{u}{1201-u}\n\\le\n401(5/7)^{100}\n&lt;\n\\frac1{100}.\n\\]\nFor the second inequality, the first hundred factors are at most\n\\(500/701&lt;5/7\\), and the remaining factors are at most one. For the last inequality, use\n\\[\n(5/7)^3&lt;\\frac12,\n\\qquad\n40100&lt;2^{33}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the field \\(K\\), integer \\(M=p^4-1\\), and element \\(\\zeta\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  P=(\\mathbb F_p)^3,\\qquad\n  A_0=(\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z)^P,\n  \\qquad\n  H=A_0\\rtimes P,\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  (s\\cdot a)(x)=a(x-s),\n  \\qquad\n  (a,s)(a',s')=(a+s\\cdot a',s+s').\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c\\in A_0\\) have value one at zero and zero elsewhere. Put\n  \\[\n  u(s)=(0,s),\\qquad\n  U=u(P),\\qquad\n  v(s)=cu(s)c^{-1},\\qquad\n  V=cUc^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\ell=\\frac{p-1}{2},\\qquad\n  D=3(p-1),\\qquad\n  w=|H|=M^{p^3}p^3.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The regular rank of an idempotent \\(e\\in K[H]\\) means\n  \\[\n  d=\\dim_K eK[H],\n  \\]\n  the rank of its left multiplication operator.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a specified idempotent \\(e\\in K[H]\\) satisfying, for every \\(s\\in P\\),\n\\[\n(u(s)-1)^\\ell e=0,\n\\qquad\ne(v(s)-1)^\\ell=0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac dw&gt;\\frac1{96}.\n\\]\nIts coefficients are obtained by finite operations in \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First record the Fourier formulas used to realize the projections in the group algebra. For a finite function group\n\\[\nA_*=(\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z)^E\n\\]\nand a character \\(\\chi:A_*\\to K^*\\), put\n\\[\np_\\chi^{A_*}\n=\n|A_*|^{-1}\\sum_{a\\in A_*}\\chi(a)^{-1}a.\n\\]\nThe denominator is invertible. Reindexing gives\n\\[\nap_\\chi^{A_*}=\\chi(a)p_\\chi^{A_*}.\n\\]\nA nontrivial character has sum zero, because translating its sum by an element on which it is nontrivial multiplies the sum by a scalar different from one. It follows that\n\\[\np_\\chi^{A_*}p_\\psi^{A_*}\n=\n\\delta_{\\chi\\psi}p_\\chi^{A_*},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_\\chi p_\\chi^{A_*}=1.\n\\]\nFor the last identity, sum coefficients over the independent character exponents: every nonzero coordinate of a nonidentity element is detected by a power of \\(\\zeta\\). These nonzero, orthogonal idempotents are independent, and their number is \\(|A_*|\\), so they form a basis of \\(K[A_*]\\).</p><p>Choose the first admissible character from every \\(P\\)-orbit containing one, and call the selected set \\(\\mathcal R\\). Each selected orbit is free and has size \\(p^3\\). These orbits cover all admissible characters. Lemma 3.2 therefore gives\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|&gt;\\frac{|A_0|}{2p^3}.\n\\]\nThis does not require admissibility to be constant on an orbit.</p><p>For \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathcal R\\) and \\(x,y\\in P\\), define\n\\[\nE^\\lambda_{xy}=p^{A_0}_{x\\lambda}u(x-y).\n\\]\nConjugation gives\n\\[\nu(s)p^{A_0}_\\chi u(s)^{-1}=p^{A_0}_{s\\chi}.\n\\]\nFourier orthogonality and freeness of the orbit imply\n\\[\nE^\\lambda_{xy}E^\\lambda_{zt}\n=\n\\delta_{yz}E^\\lambda_{xt}.\n\\]\nIndeed, the Fourier product can survive only when\n\\[\nx\\lambda=(x-y+z)\\lambda,\n\\]\nequivalently \\(y=z\\). Products between distinct selected orbits vanish.</p><p>The element\n\\[\nq_\\lambda=\\sum_{x\\in P}p^{A_0}_{x\\lambda}\n\\]\nis central: it commutes with \\(A_0\\), and \\(P\\) permutes its summands. The basis\n\\(p^{A_0}_{x\\lambda}u(s)\\) of its ideal is indexed equally by \\((x,y)\\) through \\(s=x-y\\). Thus\n\\[\nq_\\lambda K[H]\\cong\\operatorname{Mat}_{p^3}(K).\n\\]\nOn this block, \\(u(s),c,v(s)\\) act, respectively, by\n\\[\nL_s,\\qquad D_\\lambda,\\qquad D_\\lambda L_sD_\\lambda^{-1}.\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\nT_\\lambda=BN_\\lambda^{-1}B^TD_\\lambda^{-1},\n\\qquad\ne=\n\\sum_{\\lambda\\in\\mathcal R}\n\\sum_{x,y\\in P}(T_\\lambda)_{xy}E^\\lambda_{xy}.\n\\]\nSince \\(B^TD_\\lambda^{-1}B=N_\\lambda\\),\n\\[\nT_\\lambda B=B,\\qquad T_\\lambda^2=T_\\lambda.\n\\]\nIts image is exactly \\(\\operatorname{im}B\\), of dimension \\(h\\). Orthogonality of the blocks proves \\(e^2=e\\); its component on the complementary ideal is zero.</p><p>In \\(\\operatorname{Mat}_{p^3}(K)\\), left multiplication by \\(T_\\lambda\\) has image the matrices whose \\(p^3\\) columns lie in \\(\\operatorname{im}B\\), and therefore has rank \\(hp^3\\). Consequently\n\\[\nd=hp^3|\\mathcal R|,\n\\qquad\n\\frac dw\n=\n\\frac{h|\\mathcal R|}{|A_0|}\n&gt;\n\\frac{h}{2p^3}\n&gt;\n\\frac1{96}.\n\\]</p><p>By Lemma 3.1,\n\\[\n(L_s-I)^\\ell T_\\lambda=0.\n\\]\nFor the other side, retain the factors in their order:\n\\[\nB^TD_\\lambda^{-1}\n(D_\\lambda L_sD_\\lambda^{-1}-I)^\\ell\n=\nB^T(L_s-I)^\\ell D_\\lambda^{-1}\n=\n0.\n\\]\nThus the required annihilations hold on every selected block and also on the complementary ideal, where \\(e\\) is zero. All choices and inverses used here are finite operations in \\(K\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In the setting of Proposition 3, let \\((e_x)_{x\\in P}\\) be the standard basis of \\(K^P\\), and define \\(L_se_x=e_{s+x}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For the three standard generators \\(\\varepsilon_j\\) of \\(P\\), set\n  \\[\n  X_j=L_{\\varepsilon_j}-I.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) have, in the prescribed order, the columns\n  \\[\n  X_1^{n_1}X_2^{n_2}X_3^{n_3}e_0,\n  \\qquad\n  0\\le n_1,n_2,n_3&lt;p,\n  \\qquad\n  n_1+n_2+n_3\\ge D-\\ell+1.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The columns of \\(B\\) are independent. Their number is\n\\[\nh=\\binom{\\ell+2}{3},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{h}{p^3}\n=\n\\frac{(p-1)(p+1)(p+3)}{48p^3}\n&gt;\n\\frac1{48}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(s\\in P\\),\n\\[\n(L_s-I)^\\ell B=0,\n\\qquad\nB^T(L_s-I)^\\ell=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The operators \\(X_j\\) commute, and characteristic \\(p\\) gives \\(X_j^p=0\\). The vectors\n\\[\nX_1^{n_1}X_2^{n_2}X_3^{n_3}e_0,\n\\qquad\n0\\le n_1,n_2,n_3&lt;p,\n\\]\nform a basis. Indeed, every translation of \\(e_0\\) is a product of powers of \\(I+X_j\\) applied to \\(e_0\\). Thus this family spans \\(K^P\\), and its cardinality is \\(p^3\\), the dimension of \\(K^P\\). In particular, the selected columns are independent.</p><p>For every \\(s\\), the matrix \\(L_s-I\\) is a polynomial in the \\(X_j\\) with zero constant term. Multiplication by its \\(\\ell\\)-th power raises the total degree of every selected column to at least \\(D+1\\). A monomial of that degree has some exponent at least \\(p\\), so it vanishes. This proves\n\\[\n(L_s-I)^\\ell B=0.\n\\]\nApply the same assertion to \\(-s\\) and transpose. Since \\(L_{-s}^T=L_s\\), this gives the second identity.</p><p>For the count, put \\(a_j=p-1-n_j\\). The new indices are nonnegative and have sum at most \\(\\ell-1\\); their individual upper bounds are automatic because \\(\\ell-1&lt;p\\). Stars and bars gives\n\\[\nh=\\binom{\\ell+2}{3}.\n\\]\nThe displayed formula for \\(h/p^3\\) follows by substituting \\(\\ell=(p-1)/2\\). The numerator of its comparison with \\(1/48\\) differs from \\(p^3\\) by\n\\[\n3p^2-p-3&gt;0\n\\]\nfor \\(p\\ge3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(A_0,P,c,B,h\\) from Proposition 3 and Lemma 3.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Characters of \\(A_0\\) take values in \\(K^*\\). Define their \\(P\\)-action by\n  \\[\n  (s\\lambda)(a)=\\lambda((-s)\\cdot a).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a character \\(\\lambda\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\chi_x=x\\lambda,\\qquad\n  D_\\lambda=\\operatorname{diag}(\\chi_x(c):x\\in P),\n  \\qquad\n  N_\\lambda=B^TD_\\lambda^{-1}B.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Call \\(\\lambda\\) admissible when its stabilizer in \\(P\\) is trivial and \\(N_\\lambda\\) is invertible.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>More than half of the \\(|A_0|=M^{p^3}\\) characters of \\(A_0\\) are admissible.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each nonzero \\(s\\in P\\), translation partitions \\(P\\) into \\(p^2\\) cycles of length \\(p\\). A character fixed by \\(s\\) has constant exponent on each cycle, so there are exactly \\(M^{p^2}\\) such characters. A union bound therefore bounds the fraction with nontrivial stabilizer by\n\\[\n(p^3-1)M^{p^2-p^3}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(c_r\\) be the coordinate generator at \\(r\\). Since\n\\[\n\\chi_x(c)=\\lambda(c_{-x}),\n\\]\nthe diagonal entries of \\(D_\\lambda^{-1}\\) range independently over \\(K^*\\) as \\(\\lambda\\) ranges over all characters.</p><p>For independent variables \\(\\tau_x\\), Cauchy–Binet gives\n\\[\nQ(\\tau)\n:=\n\\det\\!\\bigl(B^T\\operatorname{diag}(\\tau_x)B\\bigr)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{J\\subseteq P\\\\|J|=h}}\n\\det(B_J)^2\\prod_{x\\in J}\\tau_x,\n\\]\nwhere \\(B_J\\) consists of the indicated rows in their fixed order. Some maximal minor is nonzero because \\(B\\) has full column rank. The monomials corresponding to distinct \\(J\\) are distinct. Thus \\(Q\\) is a nonzero polynomial of total degree \\(h\\).</p><p>A nonzero polynomial of total degree at most \\(a\\) vanishes on at most an \\(a/M\\) fraction of a product grid whose coordinate sets all have size \\(M\\). To prove this, induct on the number of variables. If its degree in the last variable is \\(b\\), its leading coefficient has degree at most \\(a-b\\), and vanishes on at most an \\((a-b)/M\\) fraction of the earlier choices. For every remaining choice, the last-variable polynomial has at most \\(b\\) roots, contributing at most \\(b/M\\). The constant case starts the induction.</p><p>Apply this estimate to \\(Q\\) on \\((K^*)^{p^3}\\). The singular-pairing fraction is at most \\(h/M\\). Both excluded fractions are strictly smaller than \\(1/4\\):\n\\[\n\\frac hM\\le\\frac{p^3}{p^4-1}&lt;\\frac14,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(p^3-1)M^{-p^2(p-1)}\n\\le\n\\frac{p^3-1}{M}\n&lt;\n\\frac14.\n\\]\nHere \\(p&gt;600\\); for example,\n\\[\np^4-1-4p^3=p^3(p-4)-1&gt;0.\n\\]\nTheir union therefore excludes fewer than half of the characters.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Corollary 3.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the graph \\(\\Gamma\\) from Proposition 2 and the group \\(H\\), elements \\(u(s),v(s)\\), and idempotent \\(e\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Take copies \\(H_i\\) of \\(H\\), indexed by \\(i\\in[m]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each edge \\(i&lt;j\\), identify\n  \\[\n  v(1,\\bar j,\\bar j^2)\\in H_i\n  \\quad\\text{with}\\quad\n  u(1,\\bar i,\\bar i^2)\\in H_j,\n  \\]\n  where bars denote residues in \\(\\mathbb F_p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G_0\\) be the quotient of the free product of the \\(H_i\\) by these identifications, and let \\(e_i\\) be the image of \\(e\\) from \\(K[H_i]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every edge \\(i&lt;j\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\\[\ne_ie_j=0\\quad\\text{in }K[G_0].\n\\]\nNo assertion is made about \\(e_je_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For an edge \\(i&lt;j\\), let \\(y\\) be its identified generator, initially of order \\(p\\) on either local side, and put \\(X=y-1\\). In\n\\[\nK[\\langle y\\rangle]=K[X]/(X^p),\n\\]\nthe annihilator of right multiplication by \\(X^\\ell\\) is precisely the ideal \\((X^{p-\\ell})\\).</p><p>Representatives of the left cosets \\(a\\langle y\\rangle\\) in \\(H_i\\) give a free right \\(K[\\langle y\\rangle]\\)-basis of \\(K[H_i]\\). Apply the outgoing identity \\(e_iX^\\ell=0\\) coefficientwise in this basis before passing to the quotient. It gives\n\\[\ne_i=qX^{p-\\ell}\n\\]\nfor some local \\(q\\). On the incoming side,\n\\[\nX^\\ell e_j=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(p-\\ell=\\ell+1\\),\n\\[\ne_ie_j=qX^{\\ell+1}e_j=qX(X^\\ell e_j)=0.\n\\]\nNo factor is commuted across \\(X\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, “Triangle-free gluing.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Delta\\) be a finite simple triangle-free graph.</p><p class=\"li\">At each vertex \\(i\\), let \\(D_i\\) be a finite group.</p><p class=\"li\">For every edge \\(\\gamma=\\{i,j\\}\\), let \\(C_\\gamma\\) be a nontrivial finite cyclic group with injective homomorphisms\n  \\[\n  \\iota_{\\gamma,i}:C_\\gamma\\to D_i,\n  \\qquad\n  \\iota_{\\gamma,j}:C_\\gamma\\to D_j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the following short-word condition at every vertex \\(i\\). If \\(1\\le k\\le3\\), the edges \\(\\gamma_1,\\ldots,\\gamma_k\\) are incident to \\(i\\), and \\(s_a\\in C_{\\gamma_a}\\setminus\\{1\\}\\), then\n  \\[\n  \\iota_{\\gamma_1,i}(s_1)\\cdots\n  \\iota_{\\gamma_k,i}(s_k)\\ne1\n  \\]\n  whenever consecutive edge labels are distinct cyclically. For \\(k=1\\), omit the distinctness condition; for \\(k\\ge2\\), it includes \\(\\gamma_k\\ne\\gamma_1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  Q=\n  \\left(\\mathop{*}_{i\\in V(\\Delta)}D_i\\right)\n  \\Big/\n  \\left\\langle\\!\\left\\langle\n  \\iota_{\\gamma,i}(s)\\iota_{\\gamma,j}(s)^{-1}\n  :\n  \\gamma=\\{i,j\\},\\ s\\in C_\\gamma\n  \\right\\rangle\\!\\right\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For the proof, put\n  \\[\n  D_i^0=\\langle\\iota_{\\gamma,i}(C_\\gamma):\\gamma\\ni i\\rangle.\n  \\]\n  Choose a generator of each \\(C_\\gamma\\). Let \\(L\\) have one generator \\(x_\\gamma\\) for each edge and all relators in the \\(x_\\gamma^{\\pm1}\\) incident to one vertex that evaluate to one in that vertex group.</p><p class=\"li\">A labelled spherical map is a finite connected graph drawn tamely on the sphere, allowing loops and parallel edges. An edge is neutral with value one, or labelled by an edge \\(\\gamma\\) of \\(\\Delta\\), with its two oriented darts assigned mutually inverse values in \\(C_\\gamma\\setminus\\{1\\}\\). Every face has a vertex type incident to every label in its boundary walk. Its boundary word is evaluated in that vertex group.</p><p class=\"li\">Such a map is exceptional when exactly one face has nonidentity evaluation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every natural homomorphism \\(D_i\\to Q\\) is injective.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.1 and 5.3 show that every \\(D_i^0\\to L\\) is injective.</p><p>To extend to the full vertex groups, let\n\\[\n\\Omega=L\\times\\mathbb N\n\\]\nwith \\(L\\) acting by left multiplication on the first coordinate. Its restriction to each embedded \\(D_i^0\\) is free and has countably infinitely many orbits. The same holds for the left \\(D_i^0\\)-action on \\(D_i\\times\\mathbb N\\).</p><p>Pair these orbits and choose a point in each paired orbit. Extending equivariantly gives a \\(D_i^0\\)-equivariant bijection\n\\[\nD_i\\times\\mathbb N\\longrightarrow\\Omega.\n\\]\nTransport the free left \\(D_i\\)-action along this bijection, separately for every \\(i\\). Its restriction to \\(D_i^0\\) is the original \\(L\\)-action.</p><p>The transported actions agree on every edge group, since both copies of its chosen generator act as \\(x_\\gamma\\in L\\). They therefore define an action of \\(Q\\) on \\(\\Omega\\), faithful on every \\(D_i\\). Hence every \\(D_i\\to Q\\) is injective.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the groups \\(D_i^0,L\\) and labelled spherical maps defined in Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The natural maps \\(D_i^0\\to L\\) are well-defined. If one of them is not injective, an exceptional labelled spherical map exists.</p>", "proof": "<p>Two words representing the same element of \\(D_i^0\\) differ by a defining relator of \\(L\\), so the map \\(D_i^0\\to L\\) is well-defined.</p><p>Suppose that a nonidentity element of \\(D_i^0\\) maps to one in \\(L\\), and choose a word \\(w\\) representing it. There is a finite sequence from \\(w\\) to the empty word in which each step inserts or deletes a defining relator, its inverse, or a free inverse pair. Indeed, membership in the normal closure expresses \\(w\\) in the free group as a finite product of conjugates of relators; free insertions and cancellations reach such a product, and its conjugated relators can be erased.</p><p>Draw the successive words at horizontal levels of a rectangle, with each letter an oriented edge. Represent an empty level by a single neutral edge. In each intervening strip, join corresponding vertices along the unchanged prefix and suffix by noncrossing neutral edges. At an insertion or deletion, join the insertion vertex on the shorter path to the endpoints of the inserted block on the longer path. The strip faces read either a letter followed by its inverse or the inserted relator, with neutral edges interspersed. If a level is empty, the adjacent nonempty word is the entire inserted or deleted block, and one face between them suffices.</p><p>Assign each relator face its vertex type and each unchanged-letter face either endpoint type of its label. All these faces evaluate to one. Include neutral edges along the two vertical sides. The exterior face has word \\(w\\) or \\(w^{-1}\\), apart from neutral edges; give it type \\(i\\). Viewed on the sphere, this is an exceptional map.</p><p>Identity versus nonidentity of a face evaluation is well-defined: changing the starting point conjugates the boundary product, and reversing the walk inverts it.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the labelled maps of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose an exceptional map exists, and choose one with the smallest number of edges.</p><p class=\"li\">A loop counts twice in vertex degree, and boundary lengths count dart occurrences.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>This minimal map has the following properties:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every edge is nonneutral and is not a bridge.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The two faces across every edge have distinct types, equal to the two endpoints of its label.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. There is at most one loop; if present, it bounds the exceptional face as a monogon.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. No face has consecutive nonloop darts of the same label, including the last and first darts of its cyclic boundary walk.</p>", "proof": "<p>The map has a positive number of edges, since the sole face of an isolated vertex has empty word and evaluates to one.</p><p>Fix an orientation of the sphere. At each vertex, let \\(\\sigma\\) be the cyclic successor of outgoing darts, and let \\(\\alpha\\) reverse a dart. Boundary walks are the cycles of \\(\\sigma\\alpha\\). For a disconnected drawing, use each component’s own boundary walks, counting one empty walk for an isolated vertex. If \\(v,e,F,c\\) are its numbers of vertices, edges, boundary walks, and components, then\n\\[\nv-e+F=2c.\n\\]\nFor a connected drawing, a nonbridge edge lies on a simple circuit, so its two sides are on distinct boundary walks. Deleting it merges those walks and preserves connectedness. Repeated deletion leaves a tree, with one boundary walk and \\(e=v-1\\). This proves the connected formula, and summing proves the displayed formula. The same walk rules follow from small vertex discs and thin edge bands.</p><p>A neutral nonloop edge can be contracted, omitting its neutral darts from the boundary words. Every face evaluation is preserved, contrary to minimality.</p><p>For a neutral loop based at \\(b\\), retain the closed side containing the exceptional face, and remove the loop. The retained graph is connected, since each side attaches to the other only at \\(b\\). The remaining dart slots on the retained side form an interval between the loop slots; closing it gives the old walks on that side with the neutral loop dart removed. Their evaluations are unchanged. If only \\(b\\) remained, its walk would be empty and could not be exceptional. Thus a smaller exceptional map results. There are no neutral edges.</p><p>There are no bridges. Delete a bridge of value \\(s\\), leaving two components. Its incident face walk splits into words \\(W_1,W_2\\), possibly empty, both receiving the old face type. With suitable starting point and orientation, its old word is\n\\[\nW_1sW_2s^{-1}.\n\\]\nIf it was exceptional, at least one \\(W_a\\) has nonidentity evaluation; retain that component. If it was ordinary, the evaluations of \\(W_1,W_2\\) are either both identity or both nonidentity. In the first case retain the component containing the original exceptional face. In the second retain the component without that face, whose new boundary walk is its unique exceptional face. Every choice gives a smaller exceptional map.</p><p>Every edge consequently separates two distinct faces. If their types were equal, delete the edge and merge the faces using their common type. If the old words, starting at its opposite darts, are \\(sD\\) and \\(s^{-1}E\\), then the merged word is \\(DE\\), and\n\\[\nDE=s^{-1}(sD)s(s^{-1}E).\n\\]\nThus two ordinary faces merge to an ordinary face, and an ordinary face and an exceptional face merge to an exceptional face. Minimality excludes this. The types across an edge are therefore its label’s two endpoints.</p><p>Given a loop, keep its closed side not containing the exceptional face, including the loop and its base vertex, and erase the graph on the other side. All retained old faces are ordinary. The erased side becomes a monogon with the loop’s nonidentity value, and either endpoint of its label supplies its type. This is another exceptional map. Minimality forces the erased side to have contained no other edges. It was already the exceptional monogon. This applies to every loop, and the one exceptional face cannot be a monogon for two different loops.</p><p>It remains to exclude consecutive nonloop darts of the same label. Suppose a face \\(\\Phi\\) has consecutive darts\n\\[\ne:a\\longrightarrow b,\\qquad f:b\\longrightarrow d\n\\]\nwith common label \\(\\gamma\\) and respective values \\(\\epsilon,\\phi\\). They belong to distinct edges; otherwise their edge would occur on both sides of \\(\\Phi\\) and would be a bridge. Let \\(X,Y\\) contain \\(\\bar e,\\bar f\\), respectively. They differ from \\(\\Phi\\) and have the common type \\(j\\), the other endpoint of \\(\\gamma\\).</p><p>Remove \\(e,f\\), and insert \\(g:a\\to d\\) along their arcs, smoothed through the empty corner of \\(\\Phi\\) at \\(b\\). Give \\(g\\) value \\(\\epsilon\\phi\\) and label \\(\\gamma\\), or make it neutral if \\(\\epsilon\\phi=1\\). If \\(a=d\\), this produces a loop. Retain every vertex, including isolated ones. At \\(a,d\\), the new darts occupy the old slots of \\(e,\\bar f\\); at \\(b\\), delete the consecutive slots \\(\\bar e,f\\). These instructions specify the embedding even when vertices or boundary segments coincide. The boundary of \\(\\Phi\\) replaces \\(ef\\) by \\(g\\), preserving its evaluation.</p><p>If \\(X\\ne Y\\), write\n\\[\nX=\\bar eD,\\qquad Y=\\bar fE,\n\\qquad\nD:a\\to b,\\quad E:b\\to d.\n\\]\nThe walks \\(D,E\\) avoid the removed edges, so the new graph remains connected. The two opposite faces merge to \\(\\bar gDE\\), whose evaluation in \\(D_j\\) is\n\\[\n(\\epsilon\\phi)^{-1}DE\n=\n\\phi^{-1}(\\epsilon^{-1}D)\\phi(\\phi^{-1}E).\n\\]\nIt is ordinary when both old faces were ordinary and exceptional when exactly one was exceptional. This gives a smaller exceptional map.</p><p>If \\(X=Y\\), write\n\\[\nX=\\bar eD\\bar fE,\n\\qquad\nD:a\\to d,\\quad E:b\\to b.\n\\]\nThe slot rules replace this walk by\n\\[\n\\bar gD,\\qquad E.\n\\]\nThis follows directly from \\(\\sigma\\alpha\\): the transition from \\(\\bar g\\) is the old transition from \\(\\bar e\\), and removal of the two slots at \\(b\\) joins the end of \\(E\\) to its beginning.</p><p>If \\(E\\) is empty, the old transitions were \\(e\\mapsto f\\) and \\(\\bar f\\mapsto\\bar e\\), so\n\\[\n\\sigma(\\bar e)=f,\\qquad \\sigma(f)=\\bar e.\n\\]\nThus \\(b\\) had degree two and becomes an isolated vertex with its empty face. If \\(D\\) is empty, then \\(a=d\\), and \\(\\bar gD\\) is the monogon of the new loop. Hence the description includes the empty cases.</p><p>The total number of boundary walks increases by one, the edge count decreases by one, and the vertex count is unchanged. The formula \\(v-e+F=2c\\) gives exactly two components. The new edge puts \\(a,d\\) in one component. Every component contains an endpoint of a removed edge, since the old graph was connected; hence \\(b\\) lies in the other. The two displayed walks belong to different components.</p><p>Give both walks type \\(j\\), and denote their evaluations by\n\\[\nA=(\\epsilon\\phi)^{-1}D,\\qquad B=E.\n\\]\nThe old evaluation is \\(\\epsilon^{-1}D\\phi^{-1}B\\). Cyclically moving the first factor to the end preserves identity versus nonidentity, and yields\n\\[\n(\\phi^{-1}B\\phi)A.\n\\]\nIf \\(X\\) was exceptional, at least one of \\(A,B\\) is nonidentity; retain its component. If \\(X\\) was ordinary, \\(A,B\\) are either both identity or both nonidentity. In the former case retain the component containing the original exceptional face, whose evaluation is unchanged even if it is \\(\\Phi\\). In the latter retain the component without that face; its new boundary is its sole exception. Again a smaller exceptional map results.</p><p>All four properties follow.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the short-word condition and triangle-free graph of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>No exceptional labelled spherical map exists.</p>", "proof": "<p>If one existed, choose a minimal one and apply Lemma 5.2.</p><p>At a vertex not based at a loop, reading the adjacent face types gives a closed walk in \\(\\Delta\\). Each incident edge crosses between its two distinct endpoint types. Successive labels are distinct by the smoothing reduction, so the walk has no immediate reversal. Its length is positive because the map is connected and has edges. A simple triangle-free graph has no such closed walk of length less than four. Thus this vertex has degree at least four.</p><p>An ordinary face not incident to a loop has a nonempty boundary walk: an empty walk in a connected drawing occurs only for an isolated vertex. Its consecutive labels are distinct. The short-word condition therefore forces its length to be at least four.</p><p>Euler’s formula and the two degree sums give\n\\[\n\\sum_v(4-\\deg v)+\\sum_F(4-|\\partial F|)=8.\n\\]\nWithout a loop, only the exceptional face can contribute positively, and its nonempty boundary contributes at most three.</p><p>With a loop, its base vertex contributes at most two, and the exceptional monogon contributes three. The ordinary face on the other side contributes at most two: its length cannot be one, since its sole letter would be the loop’s nonidentity value. There are no other loops, so every remaining vertex and face contributes at most zero. The total is at most\n\\[\n2+3+2=7.\n\\]\nBoth cases contradict the displayed identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 4.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G_0\\) be the free-product quotient of the copies \\(H_i\\) defined in Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">The edge identifications are\n  \\[\n  v(1,\\bar j,\\bar j^2)\\in H_i\n  \\quad=\\quad\n  u(1,\\bar i,\\bar i^2)\\in H_j\n  \\qquad(i&lt;j).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every natural map \\(H_i\\to G_0\\) is injective. Consequently every induced map\n\\[\nK[H_i]\\longrightarrow K[G_0]\n\\]\nis injective.</p>", "proof": "<p>The coordinate formula\n\\[\nv(s)=(c-s\\cdot c,s)\n\\]\ngives \\(U\\cap V=\\{1\\}\\), since \\(c-s\\cdot c\\ne0\\) for \\(s\\ne0\\).</p><p>Each identified generator has order \\(p\\), so its map from \\(C_p\\) is injective. At a fixed vertex, every incident edge subgroup lies in either \\(U\\) or \\(V\\). Within either subgroup, distinct neighbor labels \\(j\\) supply the vectors\n\\[\n(1,\\bar j,\\bar j^2).\n\\]\nA nonidentity syllable corresponds to a nonzero scalar multiple of its vector.</p><p>Any three vectors with distinct residues are independent, since their determinant is, up to sign,\n\\[\n(\\bar j_2-\\bar j_1)(\\bar j_3-\\bar j_1)(\\bar j_3-\\bar j_2)\\ne0.\n\\]\nThe residues are distinct because \\(p&gt;m\\). The first two coordinates similarly give independence for two vectors, and a single vector is nonzero. Thus a word of at most three nonidentity syllables with distinct labels, all in \\(U\\) or all in \\(V\\), cannot evaluate to one.</p><p>For a mixed word of length two or three, one of \\(U,V\\) supplies exactly one syllable. After a cyclic permutation, a putative relation equates that nonidentity syllable to a product of syllables in the other subgroup. This contradicts \\(U\\cap V=\\{1\\}\\). For lengths two and three, cyclically distinct consecutive labels are exactly distinct labels.</p><p>The graph is simple and triangle-free by Proposition 2. Proposition 5 therefore gives the injections \\(H_i\\hookrightarrow G_0\\). Distinct elements of an embedded subgroup remain distinct group-basis elements, proving the group-algebra injections.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this proposition, let \\(K\\) be any field of characteristic \\(p\\), let \\(M\\) be a positive integer prime to \\(p\\), and suppose \\(\\zeta\\in K\\) has order \\(M\\). These replace the specific field choices of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(C_M=\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z\\), and put\n  \\[\n  P=(\\mathbb F_p)^3,\\qquad\n  A_0=(C_M)^P,\\qquad\n  H=A_0\\rtimes P,\\qquad\n  w=|H|,\n  \\]\n  with translation action on \\(A_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let a countable group \\(G_0\\) contain specified copies \\(H_1,\\ldots,H_m\\) of \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(e_i\\in K[H_i]\\) be idempotents with the same positive integer regular rank\n  \\[\n  d=\\dim_K e_iK[H_i].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Introduce an independent element \\(z\\) of order \\(M\\), and set\n  \\[\n  Q_{\\mathrm{base}}=\\langle z\\rangle\\times G_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(i\\), define\n  \\[\n  B_i=(C_M)^{H_i},\n  \\qquad\n  L_i=B_i\\rtimes H_i,\n  \\qquad\n  (h\\cdot b)(x)=b(h^{-1}x).\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\delta_i\\in B_i\\) be the constant function of value one.</p><p class=\"li\">Identify\n  \\[\n  C_i=\\langle z\\rangle\\times H_i\\le Q_{\\mathrm{base}}\n  \\]\n  with its subgroup in \\(L_i\\), sending \\((z^k,h)\\) to \\((k\\delta_i,h)\\). Let \\(G_1\\) be the resulting quotient of\n  \\[\n  Q_{\\mathrm{base}}*L_1*\\cdots*L_m.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  R=K[G_1],\n  \\qquad\n  f=\\frac1M\\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\\zeta^{-k}z^k,\n  \\qquad\n  S=fR,\n  \\qquad\n  P_i=fe_i.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The group \\(G_1\\) contains \\(G_0\\), and \\(z\\) is central of exact order \\(M\\). The element \\(f\\) is a nonzero central idempotent, and \\(S\\) has identity \\(f\\).</p><p>There are explicitly specified matrices\n\\[\nJ_i\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{w\\times d}(S),\n\\qquad\nY_i\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{d\\times w}(S)\n\\]\nsatisfying\n\\[\nY_iJ_i=fI_d,\n\\qquad\nJ_iY_i=P_iI_w.\n\\]\nTheir entries lie in \\(fK[L_i]\\), the group algebra of an explicitly attached finite subgroup multiplied by \\(f\\). Their construction uses only finite group operations and linear algebra over \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 proves the required embeddings and the assertions about \\(z,f,S\\). Each \\(P_i\\) is an idempotent in \\(fK[L_i]\\). Any identity \\(e_ie_j=0\\) in \\(K[G_0]\\) remains valid after multiplication by \\(f\\).</p><p>Fix \\(i\\) and a block \\(\\theta\\) of Lemma 7.3. Use \\(s,D,E,d_0,Q,\\Pi\\) from Lemma 7.4. Order pairs lexicographically, and take the increasing bijection between\n\\[\n\\{(\\mu,l):1\\le\\mu\\le d,\\ 1\\le l\\le s\\}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\{(\\lambda,k):1\\le\\lambda\\le w,\\ 1\\le k\\le d_0\\}.\n\\]\nTheir sizes agree because \\(sd=wd_0\\).</p><p>Let \\(V\\) be the \\(w\\)-by-\\(d\\) block matrix, with \\(s\\)-by-\\(s\\) entries, sending each coordinate \\((\\mu,l)\\) to its paired coordinate \\((\\lambda,k)\\). Thus \\(V_{\\lambda\\mu}\\) is the sum of standard matrix units \\(E^{\\mathrm{std}}_{kl}\\) over those pairings. Let \\(W\\) be the \\(d\\)-by-\\(w\\) block matrix reversing each pairing, using the corresponding units \\(E^{\\mathrm{std}}_{lk}\\), and sending unpaired coordinates with \\(k&gt;d_0\\) to zero. Directly on coordinates,\n\\[\nWV=I_d\\otimes I_s,\n\\qquad\nVW=I_w\\otimes\\Pi.\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\nJ^{(\\theta)}=(I_w\\otimes Q)V,\n\\qquad\nY^{(\\theta)}=W(I_w\\otimes Q^{-1}).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nY^{(\\theta)}J^{(\\theta)}=I_d\\otimes I_s,\n\\qquad\nJ^{(\\theta)}Y^{(\\theta)}=I_w\\otimes E.\n\\]</p><p>Apply \\(\\rho^{-1}\\) entrywise to the tuples of blocks \\(J^{(\\theta)}\\) and \\(Y^{(\\theta)}\\). Lemma 7.3 sends \\(f\\) to the tuple of identities and \\(P_i\\) to the tuple of matrices \\(E\\). The resulting matrices \\(J_i,Y_i\\) satisfy\n\\[\nY_iJ_i=fI_d,\n\\qquad\nJ_iY_i=P_iI_w.\n\\]</p><p>Every choice is in a finite set: characters, cosets, augmentation matrices, and coordinate pairings. Taking first representatives and first independent columns in fixed orders specifies all choices. The inverse for \\(Q\\) is a finite sum, and \\(\\rho^{-1}\\) is the finite group-algebra formula of Lemma 7.3. Thus all entries belong to \\(fK[L_i]\\) and are obtained by finite operations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(Q_{\\mathrm{base}},L_i,C_i,G_1,z,f\\) from Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every factor \\(Q_{\\mathrm{base}},L_1,\\ldots,L_m\\) embeds in \\(G_1\\). The element \\(z\\) is central of exact order \\(M\\), and \\(f\\) is a nonzero central idempotent.</p>", "proof": "<p>The element \\(\\delta_i\\) has order \\(M\\), is fixed by \\(H_i\\), and satisfies\n\\[\n\\langle\\delta_i\\rangle\\cap H_i=1.\n\\]\nThus \\(C_i\\) embeds in \\(L_i\\) as prescribed.</p><p>Let \\(X\\) be a countable disjoint union of copies of the left regular \\(Q_{\\mathrm{base}}\\)-set. Its restriction to each \\(C_i\\) is free and has countably infinitely many orbits. A countable disjoint union \\(X_i\\) of regular \\(L_i\\)-sets has the same property on restriction to \\(C_i\\).</p><p>Pair the \\(C_i\\)-orbits and select a point in each paired orbit. Extending these pairings equivariantly gives a \\(C_i\\)-equivariant bijection \\(X\\to X_i\\). Transport the \\(L_i\\)-action to \\(X\\). The actions of all factors agree on the prescribed \\(C_i\\), and therefore define an action of \\(G_1\\). Each factor acts faithfully, proving its embedding.</p><p>The element \\(z\\) is central in every generating factor, hence in \\(G_1\\), and retains order \\(M\\) because \\(Q_{\\mathrm{base}}\\) embeds. In \\(R\\),\n\\[\nzf=\\zeta f,\n\\qquad\nf^2\n=\n\\frac1M\\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\\zeta^{-k}z^kf\n=\nf.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of the identity in \\(f\\) is \\(M^{-1}\\ne0\\), since \\(1,z,\\ldots,z^{M-1}\\) are distinct. Thus \\(f\\ne0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(i\\) in Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">For a character \\(\\chi:B_i\\to K^*\\), put\n  \\[\n  p_\\chi=\\frac1{|B_i|}\\sum_{b\\in B_i}\\chi(b)^{-1}b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the action\n  \\[\n  (h\\chi)(b)=\\chi(h^{-1}\\cdot b).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal X_i=\\{\\chi:\\chi(z)=\\zeta\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose an orbit representative \\(\\theta\\in\\mathcal X_i\\), and put\n  \\[\n  T_\\theta=\\operatorname{Stab}_{H_i}(\\theta).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The subgroup \\(T_\\theta\\) is elementary abelian of exponent \\(p\\) and rank at most three; the trivial subgroup is allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(b_x\\) be the coordinate function with value one at \\(x\\in H_i\\) and zero elsewhere, and write\n\\[\n\\theta(b_x)=\\zeta^{a_x},\n\\qquad\na_x\\in\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nSince \\(z=\\sum_x b_x\\), the condition \\(\\theta(z)=\\zeta\\) is\n\\[\n\\sum_{x\\in H_i}a_x=1\n\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z.\n\\]</p><p>Suppose a nonidentity \\(g\\in A_0\\cap T_\\theta\\) has order \\(q\\). The exponent of \\(A_0\\) divides \\(M\\), so \\(q&gt;1\\) divides \\(M\\). Left multiplication by \\(g\\) partitions \\(H_i\\) into cycles of length \\(q\\). Invariance of \\(\\theta\\) makes the \\(a_x\\) constant on these cycles. Their sum lies in \\(q(\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z)\\), and cannot equal one: reduction modulo \\(q\\) would give \\(0=1\\).</p><p>Thus \\(T_\\theta\\cap A_0=1\\). The quotient map\n\\[\nH_i\\longrightarrow H_i/A_0\\cong P\n\\]\nis injective on \\(T_\\theta\\). Every subgroup of \\((\\mathbb F_p)^3\\) is an \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-linear subspace, which proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(i\\) in Proposition 7, and use \\(\\mathcal X_i,p_\\chi\\) from Lemma 7.2.</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(H_i\\)-orbit in \\(\\mathcal X_i\\), choose its first representative \\(\\theta\\), and put\n  \\[\n  T=T_\\theta,\\qquad\n  s=[H_i:T],\\qquad\n  D=K[T].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose ordered representatives \\(r_1,\\ldots,r_s\\) of the left cosets \\(hT\\). Regard \\(K[H_i]\\) as a free right \\(D\\)-module with this basis.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\in H_i\\) act by left multiplication \\(L_g\\), and let \\(b\\in B_i\\) act by\n  \\[\n  D_b(h)=(h\\theta)(b)h\n  \\qquad(h\\in H_i).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These actions define homomorphisms\n\\[\n\\rho_\\theta:K[L_i]\\to\\operatorname{Mat}_s(D)\n\\]\nand an explicitly invertible algebra isomorphism\n\\[\n\\rho:fK[L_i]\\longrightarrow\n\\prod_\\theta\n\\operatorname{Mat}_{[H_i:T_\\theta]}(K[T_\\theta]).\n\\]\nIt sends \\(f\\) to the tuple of identity matrices. For \\(t_0\\in T_\\theta\\),\n\\[\np_{r_a\\theta}\\,r_at_0r_b^{-1}\n\\]\nis sent to the matrix with entry \\(t_0\\) in position \\((a,b)\\) of the \\(\\theta\\)-block and zero in every other block.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Fourier calculation in the proof of Proposition 3, applied to the finite index set \\(H_i\\), shows that the \\(p_\\chi\\) are a basis of orthogonal idempotents summing to one, and\n\\[\nbp_\\chi=\\chi(b)p_\\chi,\n\\qquad\nhp_\\chi h^{-1}=p_{h\\chi}.\n\\]\nSince \\(z=\\delta_i\\) is fixed by \\(H_i\\), the set \\(\\mathcal X_i\\) is invariant, and\n\\[\nf=\\sum_{\\chi\\in\\mathcal X_i}p_\\chi.\n\\]</p><p>The actions \\(L_g,D_b\\) commute with right multiplication by \\(T\\). For the diagonal action this follows from \\((ht)\\theta=h\\theta\\) for \\(t\\in T\\). They respect the semidirect-product relation:\n\\[\nL_gD_bL_g^{-1}(h)\n=\n((g^{-1}h)\\theta)(b)h\n=\n(h\\theta)(g\\cdot b)h\n=\nD_{g\\cdot b}(h).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\rho_\\theta\\) is an algebra homomorphism. The element \\(z\\) acts as \\(\\zeta\\), so \\(\\rho_\\theta(f)=I_s\\).</p><p>The element\n\\[\np_{r_a\\theta}r_at_0r_b^{-1}\n\\]\nbelongs to \\(fK[L_i]\\). In the \\(\\theta\\)-representation it sends \\(r_bt'\\) to \\(r_at_0t'\\) for every \\(t'\\in T\\), and kills every other basis coset. Indeed, its group factor sends \\(r_cT\\) to \\(r_at_0r_b^{-1}r_cT\\); the Fourier projection retains this coset exactly when it equals \\(r_aT\\), equivalently \\(c=b\\). In a representation from another orbit, the Fourier projection vanishes.</p><p>Therefore the product map \\(\\rho\\) is surjective. It is injective by dimension comparison. The elements\n\\[\np_\\chi h,\n\\qquad\n\\chi\\in\\mathcal X_i,\\ h\\in H_i,\n\\]\nform a basis of the source. An orbit of size \\(s\\) contributes \\(sw=s^2|T|\\) basis elements, equal to the dimension of its target block. Source and target have equal finite dimension.</p><p>The displayed preimages of matrix entries, extended linearly in \\(t_0\\) and summed over entries and blocks, give the explicit inverse.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix one block from Lemma 7.3, with \\(T=T_\\theta\\), \\(s=[H_i:T]\\), and \\(D=K[T]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  E=\\rho_\\theta(P_i)\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_s(D).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\varepsilon:D\\to K\\) be augmentation, let \\(\\mathfrak a=\\ker\\varepsilon\\), and write\n  \\[\n  \\bar E=\\varepsilon(E),\n  \\qquad\n  d_0=\\operatorname{rank}_K\\bar E.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an explicitly constructed invertible \\(Q\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_s(D)\\) such that\n\\[\nQ^{-1}EQ=\\Pi:=\\operatorname{diag}(I_{d_0},0).\n\\]\nMoreover, as equalities of integers,\n\\[\nd=d_0|T|,\n\\qquad\nw=s|T|,\n\\qquad\nwd_0=sd.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>As a \\(K\\)-linear operator on \\(K[H_i]\\), the matrix \\(E\\) is exactly left multiplication by \\(e_i\\), because \\(f\\) acts as the identity. Hence\n\\[\n\\dim_K\\operatorname{im}E=d.\n\\]</p><p>By Lemma 7.2, choose generators \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_q\\) of\n\\(T\\cong(C_p)^q\\), with \\(0\\le q\\le3\\). The elements \\(X_j=t_j-1\\) commute, satisfy \\(X_j^p=0\\), and generate \\(\\mathfrak a\\). Expanding \\(t_j=1+X_j\\) and counting dimensions gives\n\\[\nD\\cong\nK[X_1,\\ldots,X_q]/(X_1^p,\\ldots,X_q^p).\n\\]\nThus, for \\(D_{\\max}=3(p-1)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathfrak a^{D_{\\max}+1}=0.\n\\]\nIndeed, a monomial of degree greater than \\(q(p-1)\\) has an exponent at least \\(p\\). This includes \\(q=0\\), when \\(D=K\\) and \\(\\mathfrak a=0\\). Matrices with entries in \\(\\mathfrak a\\) have the same nilpotence bound.</p><p>Since \\(\\bar E\\) is idempotent, its image and the image of \\(I_s-\\bar E\\) are complementary subspaces of \\(K^s\\). Scan the columns of \\(\\bar E\\), retaining each that increases their span, and do the same for \\(I_s-\\bar E\\). Form \\(Q\\) from the corresponding \\(d_0\\) columns of \\(E\\), followed by the corresponding \\(s-d_0\\) columns of \\(I_s-E\\). Its augmentation \\(Q_0\\) is invertible over \\(K\\).</p><p>Regard \\(Q_0\\) as a scalar matrix over \\(D\\), and put\n\\[\nN=Q_0^{-1}(Q-Q_0).\n\\]\nIts entries lie in \\(\\mathfrak a\\), so \\(N^{D_{\\max}+1}=0\\). Since \\(Q=Q_0(I_s+N)\\),\n\\[\nQ^{-1}\n=\n\\left(\\sum_{k=0}^{D_{\\max}}(-N)^k\\right)Q_0^{-1}.\n\\]</p><p>The first \\(d_0\\) columns of \\(Q\\) are fixed by \\(E\\); the others are killed by \\(E\\). Consequently\n\\[\nEQ=Q\\Pi,\\qquad Q^{-1}EQ=\\Pi.\n\\]\nConjugation preserves the \\(K\\)-dimension of the image. Comparing with \\(\\dim_K\\operatorname{im}E=d\\) gives\n\\[\nd=d_0|T|.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(w=s|T|\\), this yields \\(wd_0=sd\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, “Triangular compression.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a nonzero unital algebra over a field \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m,t,w,d\\) be positive integers.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_i\\in S\\) be idempotents, and let \\(\\alpha_i(T),\\beta_j(T)\\in K\\), for \\(i,j\\in[m]\\) and \\(T\\in[t]\\), satisfy\n  \\[\n  F_{ij}=\\sum_{T=1}^t\\alpha_i(T)\\beta_j(T),\n  \\qquad\n  F_{ii}=1,\n  \\qquad\n  F_{ij}P_iP_j=0\\quad(i&lt;j).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  J_i\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{w\\times d}(S),\n  \\qquad\n  Y_i\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{d\\times w}(S),\n  \\]\n  with\n  \\[\n  Y_iJ_i=I_d,\n  \\qquad\n  J_iY_i=P_iI_w.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Explicit matrices\n\\[\nC^\\sharp\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{wt\\times md}(S),\n\\qquad\nD^\\sharp\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{md\\times wt}(S)\n\\]\nsatisfy\n\\[\nD^\\sharp C^\\sharp=I_{md}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Define\n\\[\n(C_0)_{T,j}=\\beta_j(T)P_j,\n\\qquad\n(D_0)_{i,T}=\\alpha_i(T)P_i.\n\\]\nTheir product has entries \\(F_{ij}P_iP_j\\). Hence\n\\[\nD_0C_0=\\mathsf P+N,\n\\qquad\n\\mathsf P=\\operatorname{diag}(P_1,\\ldots,P_m),\n\\]\nwhere \\(N\\) is strictly lower triangular. Thus \\(N^m=0\\). Every off-diagonal entry begins with \\(P_i\\) and ends with \\(P_j\\), so\n\\[\n\\mathsf P^2=\\mathsf P,\n\\qquad\n\\mathsf PN=N=N\\mathsf P.\n\\]</p><p>The finite sum\n\\[\nQ=\\mathsf P+\\sum_{k=1}^{m-1}(-N)^k\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\nQ(\\mathsf P+N)=\\mathsf P\n\\]\nby cancellation of consecutive powers. Set \\(D_1=QD_0\\). Then\n\\[\nD_1C_0=\\mathsf P.\n\\]\nFor \\(m=1\\), the sum is empty and the same identity holds.</p><p>Take block diagonals \\(C_{\\mathrm{rep}},D_{\\mathrm{rep}}\\) of \\(w\\) copies of \\(C_0,D_1\\). Using coordinates \\((\\lambda,i)\\) on \\(S^{wm}\\) and \\((\\lambda,T)\\) on \\(S^{wt}\\), their product is\n\\[\nD_{\\mathrm{rep}}C_{\\mathrm{rep}}\n=\n\\mathsf P_{\\mathrm{rep}}\n:=\n\\operatorname{diag}_{\\lambda=1}^w(\\mathsf P).\n\\]</p><p>Define \\(J:S^{md}\\to S^{wm}\\) and \\(Y:S^{wm}\\to S^{md}\\) by\n\\[\nJ_{(\\lambda,i),(j,\\mu)}\n=\n\\delta_{ij}(J_i)_{\\lambda\\mu},\n\\]\n\\[\nY_{(j,\\mu),(\\lambda,i)}\n=\n\\delta_{ij}(Y_i)_{\\mu\\lambda},\n\\qquad\n1\\le\\mu\\le d.\n\\]\nThe assumed rectangular identities give\n\\[\nYJ=I_{md},\n\\qquad\nJY=\\mathsf P_{\\mathrm{rep}}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nC^\\sharp=C_{\\mathrm{rep}}J,\n\\qquad\nD^\\sharp=YD_{\\mathrm{rep}}\n\\]\nhave the asserted shapes, and\n\\[\nD^\\sharp C^\\sharp\n=\nY\\mathsf P_{\\mathrm{rep}}J\n=\nYJYJ\n=\nI_{md}.\n\\]\nOnly associativity and the stated identities were used; the \\(P_i\\) need not commute.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 6.2, “A square inverse and its certificate.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R\\) be a unital ring, \\(f\\ne0\\) a central idempotent, and \\(S=fR\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(N&gt;r\\ge1\\) and\n  \\[\n  C\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{r\\times N}(S),\n  \\qquad\n  D\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{N\\times r}(S),\n  \\qquad\n  DC=fI_N.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\iota:S^r\\to S^N\\) include the first \\(r\\) coordinates, and let \\(\\pi:S^N\\to S^r\\) retain them; their entries are \\(f\\) and zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  A_R=\\pi D+(1-f)I_r,\n  \\qquad\n  B_R=C\\iota+(1-f)I_r.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u_*\\in S^N\\) have last coordinate \\(f\\) and all other coordinates zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The matrices satisfy\n\\[\nA_RB_R=I_r,\n\\qquad\nB_RA_R\\ne I_r.\n\\]\nMore precisely,\n\\[\nv_*:=Cu_*\\ne0,\n\\qquad\nDv_*=u_*,\n\\qquad\nA_Rv_*=0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(B_RA_R-I_r)v_*=-v_*\\ne0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Every entry of \\(C,D,\\iota,\\pi\\) belongs to \\(S\\), and is annihilated on both sides by \\(1-f\\). Since \\(\\pi\\iota=fI_r\\),\n\\[\nA_RB_R\n=\n\\pi DC\\iota+(1-f)I_r\n=\nfI_r+(1-f)I_r\n=\nI_r.\n\\]</p><p>The vector \\(u_*\\) is nonzero because \\(f\\ne0\\). Also\n\\[\nDv_*=DCu_*=u_*,\n\\]\nso \\(v_*\\ne0\\). Since \\(N&gt;r\\), one has \\(\\pi u_*=0\\), and therefore\n\\[\nA_Rv_*=\\pi Dv_*+(1-f)v_*=0.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(B_R\\) and subtracting \\(v_*\\) gives the asserted nonzero defect.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be a field.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\ge2\\) be invertible in \\(K\\), and let \\(\\zeta\\in K\\) have order \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(q\\ge0\\), put\n  \\[\n  Z_q=(\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z)^q,\n  \\qquad\n  n=M^q.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite group \\(\\Lambda\\) of order \\(Mn^2\\), a nonzero central idempotent \\(f'\\in K[\\Lambda]\\), and explicitly specified elements\n\\(\\Omega_{uv}\\), for \\(u,v\\in Z_q\\), forming a basis of \\(f'K[\\Lambda]\\), such that\n\\[\n\\Omega_{uv}\\Omega_{st}\n=\n\\delta_{vs}\\Omega_{ut},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{u\\in Z_q}\\Omega_{uu}=f'.\n\\]\nThus the standard matrix units give an isomorphism\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Mat}_n(K)\\cong f'K[\\Lambda].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Take\n\\[\n\\Lambda=Z_q\\times Z_q\\times\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z\n\\]\nwith multiplication\n\\[\n(x,y,k)(x',y',k')\n=\n(x+x',y+y',k+k'+y\\cdot x').\n\\]\nAll coordinates and the dot product are modulo \\(M\\). Associativity follows from\n\\[\ny\\cdot x'+(y+y')\\cdot x''\n=\ny'\\cdot x''+y\\cdot(x'+x'').\n\\]\nThe zero tuple is the identity, and\n\\[\n(x,y,k)^{-1}=(-x,-y,-k+y\\cdot x).\n\\]\nNo primality assumption on \\(M\\) is used.</p><p>The element \\(z'=(0,0,1)\\) is central of exact order \\(M\\). Define\n\\[\nf'=\\frac1M\\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\\zeta^{-k}(z')^k.\n\\]\nReindexing gives \\(z'f'=\\zeta f'\\), and convolution gives \\((f')^2=f'\\). Its identity coefficient is \\(M^{-1}\\ne0\\).</p><p>Write\n\\[\nX_x=(x,0,0),\\qquad Y_y=(0,y,0),\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\nD_v=\\frac1n\\sum_{y\\in Z_q}\\zeta^{-y\\cdot v}f'Y_y.\n\\]\nThe denominator is invertible. Character orthogonality is the direct identity\n\\[\n\\sum_{y\\in Z_q}\\zeta^{y\\cdot a}\n=\n\\begin{cases}\nn,&amp;a=0,\\\\\n0,&amp;a\\ne0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFor \\(a\\ne0\\), choose a nonzero coordinate \\(a_j\\). Translation of the sum by the \\(j\\)-th coordinate vector multiplies it by \\(\\zeta^{a_j}\\ne1\\), so the sum vanishes in the field. This also works when \\(a_j\\) is a zero divisor modulo the composite integer \\(M\\).</p><p>Convolution in the abelian group of the \\(Y_y\\) gives\n\\[\nD_vD_t=\\delta_{vt}D_t.\n\\]\nIndeed, the coefficient of \\(f'Y_b\\) in the product is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\zeta^{-b\\cdot t}}{n^2}\n\\sum_{y\\in Z_q}\\zeta^{y\\cdot(t-v)}.\n\\]\nSumming over \\(v\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_vD_v=f'.\n\\]</p><p>The group law gives\n\\[\nY_yX_a=(z')^{y\\cdot a}X_aY_y,\n\\qquad\nD_vX_a=X_aD_{v-a}.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\n\\Omega_{uv}=X_{u-v}D_v\n=\n\\frac1n\\sum_{y\\in Z_q}\n\\zeta^{-y\\cdot v}f'X_{u-v}Y_y.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\Omega_{uv}\\Omega_{st}\n=\nX_{u-v+s-t}D_{v-s+t}D_t\n=\n\\delta_{vs}\\Omega_{ut},\n\\]\nand their diagonal sum is \\(f'\\).</p><p>For independence, define a representation on \\(K^{Z_q}\\) by\n\\[\n\\rho(x,y,k)e_s=\\zeta^{k+y\\cdot s}e_{s+x}.\n\\]\nThe scalar in a composite is\n\\[\n\\zeta^{k+k'+y\\cdot x'+(y+y')\\cdot s},\n\\]\nso the group law proves that this is a representation. It sends \\(f'\\) to the identity and \\(D_v\\) to the coordinate projection at \\(v\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\rho(\\Omega_{uv})e_s=\\delta_{vs}e_u,\n\\]\nproving independence of the \\(n^2\\) elements \\(\\Omega_{uv}\\).</p><p>The \\(n^2\\) elements \\(f'(x,y,0)\\) span \\(f'K[\\Lambda]\\), because\n\\[\nf'(x,y,k)=\\zeta^k f'(x,y,0).\n\\]\nTheir group-basis supports have distinct \\((x,y)\\)-coordinates, so they are independent. The sector has dimension \\(n^2\\), and the matrix units are a basis.</p><p>For \\(q=0\\), the empty tuple set has one element, \\(n=1\\), \\(\\Lambda=C_M\\), and the sole matrix unit is \\(f'\\). The formulas remain valid.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal A,\\mathcal B\\) be unital rings.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Phi:\\mathcal A\\to\\mathcal B\\) be an injective ring homomorphism, not necessarily preserving identities, and put\n  \\[\n  g=\\Phi(1_{\\mathcal A}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A,B\\in\\mathcal A\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  AB=1_{\\mathcal A},\n  \\qquad\n  BA\\ne1_{\\mathcal A}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The elements\n\\[\na=1_{\\mathcal B}-g+\\Phi(A),\n\\qquad\nb=1_{\\mathcal B}-g+\\Phi(B)\n\\]\nsatisfy\n\\[\nab=1_{\\mathcal B},\n\\qquad\nba\\ne1_{\\mathcal B}.\n\\]\nThe idempotent \\(g\\) need not be central in \\(\\mathcal B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The element \\(g\\) is idempotent and is an identity on the image of \\(\\Phi\\) on both sides. Thus \\(1-g\\) annihilates that image on both sides. Expanding gives\n\\[\nab=1-g+\\Phi(AB)=1_{\\mathcal B},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nba-1_{\\mathcal B}\n=\n\\Phi(BA-1_{\\mathcal A})\\ne0.\n\\]\nOnly the last inequality uses injectivity; no global centrality is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Corollary 7.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be a field, let \\(M\\ge2\\) be invertible in \\(K\\), and let \\(\\zeta\\in K\\) have order \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be a group and \\(r\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  A,B\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_r(K[L]),\n  \\qquad\n  AB=I_r,\n  \\qquad\n  BA\\ne I_r.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Finite formulas specify a finite group \\(\\Lambda\\) and elements\n\\[\na,b\\in K[L\\times\\Lambda]\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nab=1,\\qquad ba\\ne1.\n\\]\nIn particular, over any fixed finite field with at least three elements, a failure of stable finiteness transfers to a scalar failure after adjoining a finite direct factor, without changing the field.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the least \\(q\\ge0\\) with \\(n=M^q\\ge r\\). This is a finite search, since\n\\[\nM^r\\ge2^r\\ge r.\n\\]\nPad the matrices by identities:\n\\[\n\\widetilde A=\\operatorname{diag}(A,I_{n-r}),\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde B=\\operatorname{diag}(B,I_{n-r}).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\widetilde A\\widetilde B=I_n,\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde B\\widetilde A-I_n\n=\n\\operatorname{diag}(BA-I_r,0)\\ne0.\n\\]</p><p>Use the group and matrix units from Proposition 10, and identify\n\\[\nK[L\\times\\Lambda]=K[L]\\otimes_KK[\\Lambda].\n\\]\nFor \\(T\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_n(K[L])\\), set\n\\[\n\\Phi(T)=\\sum_{u,v\\in Z_q}T_{uv}\\otimes\\Omega_{uv}.\n\\]\nThe matrix-unit identities prove multiplicativity, and\n\\[\n\\Phi(I_n)=1\\otimes f'.\n\\]\nIndependence of the \\(\\Omega_{uv}\\) proves injectivity: extend their coordinate dual functionals to \\(K[\\Lambda]\\), and apply them in the second tensor factor to recover each \\(T_{uv}\\). This works even when \\(K[L]\\) has zero divisors.</p><p>Proposition 11 now gives\n\\[\na=\n1\\otimes(1-f')\n+\n\\sum_{u,v\\in Z_q}\\widetilde A_{uv}\\otimes\\Omega_{uv},\n\\]\n\\[\nb=\n1\\otimes(1-f')\n+\n\\sum_{u,v\\in Z_q}\\widetilde B_{uv}\\otimes\\Omega_{uv}.\n\\]\nTheir product is one, and\n\\[\nba-1\n=\n\\Phi(\\widetilde B\\widetilde A-I_n)\\ne0.\n\\]</p><p>For a finite field of order \\(Q\\ge3\\), take \\(M=Q-1\\) and a generator of its multiplicative group. Then \\(M\\ge2\\) is invertible in the field, so the construction applies.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [2, 4, "cite"], [2, 13, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 12, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [4, 16, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [7, 0, "cite"], [7, 12, "cite"], [8, 12, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 8, "cite"], [12, 0, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [16, 13, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 13, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [20, 22, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"]]});
